le: the cart service might be using the wrong hostname or port to connect to Redis. The service name in Kubernetes is usually used as the DNS name, so if the cart service is configured to connect to "valkey-cart" but the actual service is named differently, that could cause a connection failure. But earlier, the user checked the services and saw valkey-cart listed. \n\nThey should also check the metrics for Redis. If Redis is not responding, the metrics might show high latency or errors. Using the get_metrics tool with a query related to Redis could help. For example, checking if Redis is accepting connections or if there are any errors in its metrics. \n\nAlternatively, the cart service might have a configuration issue where it\'s not correctly pointing to the Redis service. Checking the cart service\'s configuration, like environment variables or config maps, could reveal if the Redis host or port is misconfigured. \n\nSince the user has access to kubectl, they could describe the cart service to see its endpoints and configuration. Also, checking the Redis pod\'s logs for any errors related to connection attempts or configuration issues would be helpful. \n\nAnother possibility is that the Redis service is not properly set up as a StatefulSet or Deployment, leading to issues with stable storage or networking. But since the pod is running, that might not be the case. \n\nIn summary, the next steps should involve checking the Redis pod\'s logs, verifying the Redis service configuration, and ensuring that the cart service is correctly configured to connect to Redis. Using tools like kubectl logs on the Redis pod and checking the service\'s YAML configuration would be the next logical steps.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe Redis service (`valkey-cart`) has an endpoint, but I need to confirm if the Redis pod itself is healthy and accepting connections. If the Redis pod is crashing or not listening on port 6379, it would cause the "connection failed" errors in the cart service. I\'ll check the Redis pod logs to identify any issues. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs valkey-cart-6c4f7cf8f6-jkksc -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=721, prompt_tokens=6706, total_tokens=7427, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9b-828d-77a2-924e-1181a610579c-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6706, 'output_tokens': 721, 'total_tokens': 7427}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8be28e45-72e6-4ef8-a52a-79cff67703ca')]]
