The root cause of the failure is that the Redis (Valkey) server in the `valkey-cart` pod is not listening on all network interfaces, only on `127.0.0.1`. This prevents the `cart` service from connecting to it, as the cart service is attempting to reach Redis over the network (e.g., via the service name `valkey-cart`). The Redis container's default configuration binds to `127.0.0.1`, which restricts access to localhost, leading to "connection failed" errors in the cart service's health checks. To resolve this, the Redis server must be configured to bind to `0.0.0.0` to accept connections from other pods in the cluster. - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8e310b3-44f3-43fa-9b8b-03a56aafb6ca'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7b507a69-f840-4900-a2a0-61191c3216cb'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='258e8392-3308-4ae6-9708-59c7ad9dcb16'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9a-813e-70e0-a728-f00c012044b8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='350ffd8c-72e5-4407-b657-c8edf609e2be'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster, specifically in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The user mentioned using the get_services tool, which retrieves service names from Grafana. \n\nWait, but the tool description says it gets the list from Grafana. However, the application in question is the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is in the astronomy-shop namespace. Should I specify the namespace when using get_services? The tool's parameters don't mention a namespace argument. Hmm, maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to that namespace, or perhaps the tool doesn't require it. The user's initial instructions say to check the namespace, so maybe I need to confirm that.\n\nBut the get_services tool's parameters are empty, so it doesn't take any arguments. That means it might return all services in the cluster, not just the astronomy-shop namespace. That could be a problem. Wait, maybe the Grafana instance the tool is querying is already filtered to the correct namespace. Alternatively, maybe I need to use kubectl to list the services in the namespace first. But the user hasn't mentioned using kubectl yet. \n\nWait, the user's initial setup says the application belongs to the astronomy-shop namespace. So perhaps the get_services tool, when called, will return services specific to that namespace. But since the tool's parameters don't include a namespace, maybe it's designed to work within that context. Alternatively, maybe I need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the services in the namespace. For example, running 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nBut the user's first step was to use get_services. Maybe the Grafana instance is already set up to show services in that namespace. However, if the tool doesn't take a namespace parameter, it might not be accurate. But since the user instructed to start with get_services, I should proceed with that. \n\nSo the tool call would be to get_services with no arguments. The response would give a list of services. Then, based on that list, I can proceed to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, etc. \n\nAlternatively, if the get_services tool isn't scoped to the namespace, the list might include services from other namespaces, which would complicate things. But given the user's setup, perhaps the Grafana instance is already configured for the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nIn any case, the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. Then, once I have the services, I can move on to checking their metrics, traces, and dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ba194caef842a0f1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9a-94f2-75d0-80c5-1c15b06491d3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ba194caef842a0f1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 3052}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'cart\', \'fraud-detection\', \'recommendation\', \'product-catalog\', \'shipping\', \'email\', \'payment\', \'currency\', \'frontend\', \'checkout\', \'load-generator\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'image-provider\', \'product-reviews\', \'ad\', \'quote\', \'accounting\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='9f972f6b-9024-47ce-bdf0-6581461474ea', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ba194caef842a0f1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='dd7147e5-8d1b-4db0-807b-f18a2c779c57'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which has many microservices. The goal is to find out if there are any faults and identify their root causes. \n\nFirst, they used the get_services tool to list all the services in the astronomy-shop namespace. The response gave a list of services like cart, fraud-detection, recommendation, etc. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be having issues.\n\nSince the application is a microservices architecture, a problem in one service could affect others. The user needs to check the health of each service. The get_traces tool can help here by looking at Jaeger traces for each service. Traces can show if there are any errors or delays in the service\'s operations. \n\nBut wait, the user might not know which specific service is faulty. So maybe they should start by checking the overall health using metrics. The get_metrics tool could be used with PromQL queries to check for errors, latency, or other anomalies. For example, looking at error rates or request latencies across all services.\n\nAlternatively, the get_dependency_graph tool could show how services are interconnected. If there\'s a service that\'s causing a lot of failures in others, that might be the root cause. But without knowing which service is problematic, it\'s a bit broad.\n\nAnother approach is to check the deployments and pods for each service. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployments" and "kubectl get pods" in the astronomy-shop namespace could reveal if any deployments are not in a healthy state, or if there are crashes in pods. \n\nBut the user might not have access to the actual cluster details yet. The initial step was to list the services, so now they need to check each service\'s status. Maybe start with checking the metrics for each service to see if any are showing high error rates or other issues. \n\nWait, the get_operations tool could also be useful. It retrieves operations for a specific service from Grafana. If a service has operations that are failing or taking too long, that could indicate a problem. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t checked any metrics or traces yet. The get_traces tool requires specifying a service and a time range. Since the user doesn\'t know which service is faulty, maybe they should start by checking the most critical services first, like the frontend, payment, or cart services, which are often key points of failure. \n\nAlternatively, using get_dependency_graph with a time range could show if there are any unusual dependencies or failures in the network between services. \n\nHmm, but the user needs to be methodical. Starting with checking the health of each service\'s deployments and pods would be a good next step. Using kubectl to get deployments and pods in the namespace can show if any services have replicas not running or pods in error states. \n\nSo, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployments" and "kubectl get pods" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would give an overview of the current state of all deployments and pods, helping identify any that are not in a healthy state. \n\nBut the user can only call one tool per step. Since the previous step was get_services, the next logical step is to check the deployments and pods. So, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployments" would be the next tool to call. That way, they can see if any deployments are in a failed state or have issues with their replicas.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo assess the current state of the application, I need to check the deployments and pods in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. Using `kubectl get deployments` will show if any deployments are in a non-healthy state (e.g., deployment failures, scaling issues). This will help identify potential root causes like misconfigured replicas, rollout failures, or resource constraints.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 806, 'prompt_tokens': 1724, 'total_tokens': 2530, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9a-b0b4-7481-a010-2ea850ba5e7a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1724, 'output_tokens': 806, 'total_tokens': 2530}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3e83d188-4b0b-4482-a9e8-7dd2c3b63450'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which returned a list of services in the astronomy-shop namespace. Now, the next logical step is to check the deployments and pods to see their current status.\n\nThe tool exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows running read-only kubectl commands. The available commands include 'kubectl get deployments' and 'kubectl get pods', which are essential for checking the state of deployments and pods. Since the user mentioned checking deployments and pods, I should use the 'kubectl get deployments' command first to see if any deployments are in a non-healthy state. \n\nWait, but the user's previous reasoning mentioned checking deployments and pods. So maybe I should run both? However, the tool call requires a single command. The user's example in the previous step used 'kubectl get deployments', so perhaps starting with that is better. Alternatively, maybe 'kubectl get all' to get a broad view, but that might be too much. \n\nLooking at the available commands, 'kubectl get deployments' is a specific and relevant command. So the correct tool call would be to execute 'kubectl get deployments' in the astronomy-shop namespace. But wait, the command needs to be a string starting with 'kubectl'. The available commands include 'kubectl get deployments', so the argument should be 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop' to specify the namespace. \n\nWait, does the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool allow specifying the namespace? The description says the command should start with 'kubectl', and the available commands include 'kubectl get'. So adding the -n flag for namespace is probably necessary. Therefore, the command should be 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nSo the tool call would be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command argument set to 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop'. That should retrieve the deployments in the specified namespace, allowing me to check their status for any issues like failed deployments or incorrect replica counts.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-88556de92d6c839b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 452, 'prompt_tokens': 2709, 'total_tokens': 3161, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9a-d547-7540-bb1e-e343dd820789-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-88556de92d6c839b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2709, 'output_tokens': 452, 'total_tokens': 3161}), ToolMessage(content='NAME              READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\naccounting        1/1     1            1           2m16s\nad                1/1     1            1           2m16s\ncart              1/1     1            1           2m16s\ncheckout          1/1     1            1           2m16s\ncurrency          1/1     1            1           2m16s\nemail             1/1     1            1           2m16s\nflagd             1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfraud-detection   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfrontend          1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfrontend-proxy    1/1     1            1           2m16s\ngrafana           1/1     1            1           2m16s\nimage-provider    1/1     1            1           2m16s\njaeger            1/1     1            1           2m16s\nkafka             1/1     1            1           2m16s\nllm               1/1     1            1           2m16s\nload-generator    1/1     1            1           2m16s\npayment           1/1     1            1           2m16s\npostgresql        1/1     1            1           2m16s\nproduct-catalog   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nproduct-reviews   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nquote             1/1     1            1           2m16s\nrecommendation    1/1     1            1           2m16s\nshipping          1/1     1            1           2m16s\nvalkey-cart       1/1     1            1           2m16s\n', id='48066ca8-ebd1-48f2-8fbb-80d3d9945914', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-88556de92d6c839b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b79e619d-01f0-4efa-9e87-6477023c7a8a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which has many microservices. So far, they\'ve used get_services to list all the services and exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the deployments. All deployments are showing as healthy (READY, UP-TO-DATE, AVAILABLE). \n\nNow, since the deployments are all okay, the next step is to check the pods themselves. Maybe some pods are not running correctly, or there are issues with their resources. The next logical step would be to check the pods in the namespace. Using kubectl get pods would show the status of each pod. If any pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar state, that could indicate a problem.\n\nAlternatively, since the user has access to metrics via Prometheus, they could check for any anomalies in resource usage (CPU, memory) or errors. But since the initial check on deployments was okay, maybe the issue is at the pod level or with the services. \n\nAnother angle is to look into the service dependencies. Using get_dependency_graph could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. But first, it\'s better to confirm if the pods are all running without issues. So, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to check pod statuses. If all pods are running, then maybe check logs or metrics next. But since the user can only use up to 20 rounds, they need to be efficient. Let\'s go with checking the pods first.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo ensure all microservices are running correctly, I need to verify the status of individual pods in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. While deployments appear healthy, pods might have issues like crashes, resource constraints, or startup failures. Checking pod statuses will confirm if any are in a non-healthy state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error). This step is critical to identify runtime issues not visible at the deployment level. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 451, 'prompt_tokens': 2406, 'total_tokens': 2857, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9a-ece8-7d70-97ce-74e1f5fb1c78-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2406, 'output_tokens': 451, 'total_tokens': 2857}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ddbc36c3-953a-4883-b00d-7cb068be8203'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. They've already checked the deployments and found all of them in a healthy state (READY, UP-TO-DATE, AVAILABLE). Now they need to check the individual pods to make sure there are no issues. The next step is to use the `kubectl get pods` command in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This will show the status of each pod, like if any are crashing or have errors. If all pods are running normally, then the next step would be to check metrics or traces for performance issues. But since the user hasn't mentioned any specific errors yet, checking the pods is the logical next step. So the tool call should be to execute `kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop`.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93b2a669ecfd0ea2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 197, 'prompt_tokens': 3434, 'total_tokens': 3631, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe9b-0235-7200-9ec3-2204ce5beee6-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93b2a669ecfd0ea2', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3434, 'output_tokens': 197, 'total_tokens': 3631}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS      AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-mnzhm        1/1     Running   0             2m24s\nad-6f9964ffd8-9s8st                1/1     Running   0             2m25s\ncart-6f59c97dd6-ldxjm              0/1     Running   0             40s\ncheckout-648b497649-7d7xv          1/1    